{
  "title": "Chapter 28: Ocean Currents",
  "order": 225,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Ocean currents are continuous, directed movements of seawater generated by planetary winds, Earth's rotation, density differences, and gravitational forces. They function as Earth's heat distribution system, transferring warm water from the tropics toward higher latitudes and bringing cold water toward equatorial regions. Without ocean currents, global climate would be far more extreme.",
      "id": "jndgag5"
    },
