    {
      "id": "0xm42ye",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning and Characteristics of Ocean Currents"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Ocean currents act as Earth's heat distribution system, transferring warm water toward high latitudes and cold water toward the equator.",
        "Coriolis force, winds, and landmasses deflect currents to form circular systems called gyres.",
        "The Indian Ocean current system is unique because it reverses seasonally due to monsoon winds.",
        "Upwelling brings cold, nutrient-rich water to the surface, creating highly productive fishing grounds like Peru and Benguela.",
        "Thermohaline circulation is driven by temperature and salinity differences that dictate deep water movements."
      ],
      "id": "ec580vv"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Ocean currents are large-scale horizontal flows of ocean water.",
      "id": "0zftidw"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• They behave like rivers within the ocean.",
      "id": "rd2x2n8"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Speed is generally higher at the surface and slower in deep waters.",
      "id": "5uux03n"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<img src=\"/books/Precis/Cluster_079_RRB-GK-Geography/images/image_c7fu25o.png\"/>• Direction is influenced by Coriolis force.",
      "id": "0guklza"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "• Currents are deflected by continental margins creating circular patterns called gyres.",
      "id": "466qry3"
    },
    {
